WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.703
One pool of progenitors makes a few early neurons, then many later ones.

2
00:00:08.463 --> 00:00:09.463
How?

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.007
In this simple model, every division gives two daughters.

4
00:00:14.007 --> 00:00:17.727
Each daughter either divides again, or leaves the cycle to become a neuron.

5
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:24.150
The fraction that keeps dividing is called P. The fraction that leaves is Q, the

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.830
leaving fraction. Together they make one.

7
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:29.093
The rules are on screen.

8
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:34.373
The pool after a cycle depends on P. The neurons made depend on Q.

9
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:39.737
Walk 100 progenitors through three cycles, with Q rising each time.

10
00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:41.597
Assume no cells die.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:48.490
When Q is 0.3, 100 progenitors make 60 neurons and 140 progenitors.

12
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.350
The pool grows.

13
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:55.943
When Q is 0.5, 140 progenitors make 140 of each.

14
00:00:56.803 --> 00:00:57.803
The pool holds still.

15
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:02.517
When Q is 0.7, they make 196 neurons.

16
00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:06.397
Only 84 progenitors remain, so the pool shrinks.

17
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:13.780
In 1996, Takao Takahashi, Richard Nowakowski and Verne Caviness measured Q in the

18
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:14.780
mouse cortex.

19
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.300
Q was low early. It crossed 0.5 on E14, in the eighth cycle.

20
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:25.343
It rose to about 0.8 by E16.

21
00:01:26.423 --> 00:01:28.203
Their model spanned eleven cell cycles.

22
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:31.993
The model covers the ventricular zone only.

23
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:37.213
Intermediate progenitors in the subventricular zone add divisions of their own.

24
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:43.023
Predict. The pool stops growing in the cycle where Q reaches one half.

25
00:01:43.863 --> 00:01:45.603
Is that when the most neurons are made?

26
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.147
No. Look back at the table.

27
00:01:54.987 --> 00:01:57.627
The next cycle makes more neurons from the same pool.

28
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:04.290
In this rising example, the pool shrinks slowly just past 0.5 while Q climbs, so

29
00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.890
output rises one more cycle.

30
00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:14.170
In general, output rises only if 2 × (1 − this Q) × next Q beats this Q. Later the

31
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.170
shrinking pool wins.

32
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:21.403
In the simulation's rising sketch, output peaks in the ninth cycle, the one after Q

33
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:22.403
reached 0.5.

34
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:27.287
About 70 percent of its output comes once Q has reached 0.5.

35
00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:30.427
The exact peak depends on how Q rises.

36
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:37.677
So the cortex first builds its pool, then spends much of it.

37
00:02:38.517 --> 00:02:41.017
Neurons spent later go to more superficial layers.

38
00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:46.457
The sketch stops at cycle 11 with progenitors left; it does not model how

39
00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:47.477
neurogenesis ends.

40
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.660
But the model counts how many daughters leave, not which identity they take.

41
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.940
Do not read it as layer sizes.

42
00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:58.073
Try the simulation below this film.

43
00:02:59.093 --> 00:03:00.413
Find the cycle with the most neurons.

44
00:03:01.517 --> 00:03:05.757
The takeaway: below 0.5 the pool grows, and above it the pool shrinks.

45
00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:09.857
Output can keep rising a cycle after the pool stops growing.
